---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="VPN"
    categorySlug="vpn"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Your Office Network Left the Building</h2>
  <p>
    Remember when everyone sat in the same building? The IT team had one job. Keep the office network locked down.
    Firewalls, monitoring, the whole thing. Nobody worried about Karen from accounting connecting from a Starbucks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those days are gone.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now your team's scattered. <a href="/blog/ultimate-home-office-setup">Home offices</a>. Coffee shops. That guy who
    keeps working from <a href="/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads">Bali</a> for some reason. Every
    laptop connecting from random WiFi is a potential hole in your security. Client data, source code, internal <a
      href="/blog/best-team-communication-tools">Slack</a>
    drama. All flying across networks you've never heard of.
  </p>
  <p>
    Business VPN fixes this. Extends your security perimeter to... everywhere, basically. But here's the thing: business
    VPNs aren't the same as the <a href="/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads">NordVPN your nephew uses</a> to watch Netflix
    abroad. Different animal entirely.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spent months evaluating these things. Deployed a few. Dealt with the headaches. Here's what actually matters.
  </p>

  <!-- Business vs Consumer VPNs -->
  <h2 id="business-vs-consumer">Business VPN vs Consumer VPN</h2>
  <p>
    Quick version: consumer VPNs protect individuals. Business VPNs protect organizations.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Management:</strong> Consumer. Everyone manages their own stuff. Business. One admin dashboard. Control
    everyone. See everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Access Control:</strong> Consumer. Can't limit what people access once connected. Business.Sarah gets the
    database. Mike doesn't. That granular.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Identity:</strong> Consumer. Yet another <a href="/tools/best-password-manager-remote-work">username and
    password</a> to remember. Business.SSO. Same Google/Okta
    login they already use.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Compliance:</strong> Consumer. No logs. Privacy feature. Business. Detailed logs. Audit trail. SOC 2
    requirements. You know.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Infrastructure:</strong> Consumer. Sharing servers with random strangers. Business. Your own dedicated
    gateways.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Which do you need?</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Consumer: Solo freelancer, tiny team, no compliance stuff, nothing sensitive to protect.
  </p>
  <p>
    Business: Internal tools exist. Auditors ask questions. You'd be in trouble if someone got in.
  </p>

  <!-- Quick Comparison -->
  <h2 id="quick-comparison">Business VPN Quick Comparison</h2>
  <p>
    Here's how the top solutions compare on key criteria:
  </p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={quickCompareColumns}
      rows={quickCompareRows}
      caption="Business VPN solutions at a glance"
  />

  <!-- Top Business VPN Solutions -->
  <h2 id="top-solutions">These Actually Work</h2>

  <!-- NordLayer -->
  <h3 id="nordlayer">NordLayer</h3>
  <p>
    The one I recommend to most companies.
  </p>
  <p>
    NordLayer started as NordVPN's business arm. Makes sense. They already knew VPN infrastructure. Now it's its own
    thing with actual enterprise features.
  </p>
  <p>
    Setup took my team about three hours. Not three weeks. Three hours. Had 40 people connected same day. The admin
    panel isn't confusing. SSO just worked with our Google Workspace.
  </p>
  <p>
    What I liked:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Zero-trust stuff without the headache</li>
    <li>Everyone uses their existing Google login</li>
    <li>Dedicated servers. Our traffic stays ours</li>
    <li>Device checks before connecting</li>
    <li>SOC 2 Type 2 already done</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    $8/user/month. 14-day trial. Minimum 5 users.
  </p>
  <p>
    For 5-500 people who need real security but don't have a dedicated IT department? This is the one.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/nordlayer"
      text="Try NordLayer Free . 14-Day Trial"
      variant="primary"
      program="nordlayer"
  />

  <!-- Perimeter 81 -->
  <h3 id="perimeter-81">Perimeter 81</h3>
  <p>
    The "we have complex infrastructure" option.
  </p>
  <p>
    Multiple offices? AWS and Azure and GCP all at once? Different teams needing access to different things? Perimeter
    81 handles it.
  </p>
  <p>
    They basically invented the software-defined perimeter concept. Lets you create invisible networks around specific
    resources. Engineering sees their stuff. Sales sees their stuff. Nobody sees everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    Solid for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Network segmentation that actually makes sense</li>
    <li>Multi-cloud setups</li>
    <li>HIPAA and PCI compliance (they have the paperwork)</li>
    <li>Organizations outgrowing simpler tools</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    $8/user/month starts it. Scales to thousands.
  </p>
  <p>
    The learning curve is steeper. Worth it if you need the depth. Overkill for a 20-person startup.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/perimeter81"
      text="Get Perimeter 81 Demo"
      variant="primary"
      program="perimeter81"
  />

  <!-- Cisco AnyConnect -->
  <h3 id="cisco-anyconnect">Cisco AnyConnect</h3>
  <p>
    Fortune 500 stuff.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're reading this for a large corporation, someone's probably already mentioned AnyConnect. It's the enterprise
    standard. Government agencies use it. Banks use it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why? Deep Cisco integration. If you're already running Cisco firewalls, Duo MFA, Umbrella DNS.AnyConnect slots right
    in. Everything talks to everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reality check: It's complex. Deployment takes weeks minimum. You need people who know what they're doing. Pricing
    requires talking to sales.
  </p>
  <p>
    For 500+ employees with existing Cisco infrastructure? Makes sense.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a 50-person startup? Overkill by a mile.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/cisco-anyconnect"
      text="Contact Cisco for Pricing"
      variant="secondary"
      program="cisco"
  />

  <!-- OpenVPN -->
  <h3 id="openvpn">OpenVPN Access Server</h3>
  <p>
    The self-hosted option.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your traffic never touches anyone else's servers. Period. You run it. You control it. Complete sovereignty.
  </p>
  <p>
    Good if:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Data can't leave your infrastructure. At all.</li>
    <li>You have DevOps people anyway</li>
    <li>Existing servers sitting around</li>
    <li>Budget is tight but labor is available</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Bad if:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>You want minimal maintenance</li>
    <li>Security isn't someone's actual job</li>
    <li>You need it working by Friday</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Free for 2 connections. $15/connection one-time after that. Plus whatever your server costs.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/openvpn"
      text="Get OpenVPN Access Server"
      variant="secondary"
      program="openvpn"
  />

  <!-- Twingate -->
  <h3 id="twingate">Twingate</h3>
  <p>
    Zero-trust done right.
  </p>
  <p>
    Twingate isn't really a VPN. It's... different. Instead of routing everything through a tunnel, it creates direct
    encrypted connections to specific resources. Only what you need. Nothing else.
  </p>
  <p>
    Users don't even notice it. No "connect to VPN" step. They just access stuff and Twingate handles it in the
    background.
  </p>
  <p>
    The architecture is genuinely clever:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>No network-level access. Just apps.</li>
    <li>Split tunneling by default</li>
    <li>Internal stuff never exposed to the internet</li>
    <li>Device trust verification every time</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Free up to 5 users. $5/user/month after. No minimums.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tech companies love this. Modern architecture. Security people approve.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/twingate"
      text="Try Twingate Free . Up to 5 Users"
      variant="primary"
      program="twingate"
  />

  <!-- GoodAccess -->
  <h3 id="goodaccess">GoodAccess</h3>
  <p>
    The scrappy European option.
  </p>
  <p>
    Startup in Prague. Aggressive pricing. Features that compete with the big names.
  </p>
  <p>
    Popular with EU companies because: GDPR, European data centers, they actually care about that stuff.
  </p>
  <p>
    $7/user/month. Dedicated IPs included. Startup discounts if you ask.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not as polished as NordLayer. Support isn't 24/7. But the value is real.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/goodaccess"
      text="Start GoodAccess Free Trial"
      variant="secondary"
      program="goodaccess"
  />

  <!-- Cloudflare Access -->
  <h3 id="cloudflare-access">Cloudflare Access</h3>
  <p>
    For teams already on Cloudflare.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cloudflare's network is insane. 300+ cities. If your team's already using Cloudflare for DNS or CDN, adding Access
    is seamless.
  </p>
  <p>
    Free for up to 50 users. Actually free. No catch except feature limits.
  </p>
  <p>
    The WARP client adds device security. Gateway filtering blocks threats. Everything runs on that massive network so
    it's fast everywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    Technical teams dig it. Integrates with their other Cloudflare stuff. Non-technical teams might find it less
    intuitive than NordLayer.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/cloudflare-access"
      text="Start Cloudflare Access Free"
      variant="secondary"
      program="cloudflare"
  />

  <!-- Quick Picks -->
  <h2 id="quick-picks">Quick Picks</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Small team (5-100):</strong> NordLayer. Fast setup. Good features. Reasonable price.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Complex infrastructure:</strong> Perimeter 81. The depth is there.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Zero-trust focused:</strong> Twingate. The architecture is sound.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Budget conscious:</strong> GoodAccess or Cloudflare free tier.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Cisco shop:</strong> AnyConnect. It's what you'd expect.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Control freaks:</strong> OpenVPN self-hosted. Nobody touches your data.
  </p>

  <!-- Feature Comparison -->
  <h2 id="feature-comparison">Feature Comparison Table</h2>
  <p>
    Here's how each solution compares on critical enterprise features (✅ = full support, ⚠️ = partial/limited,
    ❌ = not available):
  </p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={featureColumns}
      rows={featureRows}
      caption="Business VPN feature comparison"
  />

  <!-- Pricing Comparison -->
  <h2 id="pricing-comparison">Pricing Breakdown by Team Size</h2>
  <p>
    Monthly costs vary significantly based on your team size. Here's what you'd pay at different scales:
  </p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={pricingColumns}
      rows={pricingRows}
      caption="Business VPN monthly costs by team size"
  />
  <p>
    <strong>*OpenVPN self-hosted:</strong> While the software is free/cheap, factor in server costs
    ($20-100+/month), DevOps time for maintenance, and security responsibility.
  </p>

  <!-- Implementation Guide -->
  <h2 id="implementation-guide">Actually Deploying This</h2>

  <h3>Week 1-2: Figure out what you need</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>What needs protecting? Internal tools, databases, staging servers?</li>
    <li>Who needs access to what?</li>
    <li>Any compliance stuff? SOC 2? HIPAA?</li>
    <li>Trial 2-3 options with a pilot group</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Day 1-2: Set it up</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Connect SSO (Google Workspace, Okta, whatever)</li>
    <li>Deploy gateways to your cloud stuff</li>
    <li>Create access policies by role</li>
    <li>Test with admins first</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Week 3-4: Roll it out</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Tell people why (they'll complain less)</li>
    <li>Start with tech-savvy folks</li>
    <li>Write a guide for common questions</li>
    <li>Set up a support channel</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Ongoing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Quarterly access reviews</li>
    <li>Watch logs for weird stuff</li>
    <li>Add VPN setup to onboarding</li>
    <li>Revoke access same day when people leave</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Security Compliance -->
  <h2 id="compliance">Compliance Notes</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>SOC 2:</strong> NordLayer, Perimeter 81, Twingate, Cloudflare all certified.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>HIPAA:</strong> NordLayer, Perimeter 81, Cisco offer BAAs.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>GDPR:</strong> Everyone supports it. Check where data actually lives.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>PCI DSS:</strong> Perimeter 81 and Cisco specifically address this.
  </p>
  <p>
    Always ask for current certifications. Things change.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    For most companies: NordLayer. It works. Setup isn't painful. Pricing is reasonable. Support is decent.
  </p>
  <p>
    Growing fast with complex needs? Perimeter 81.
  </p>
  <p>
    Security architecture matters more than convenience? Twingate.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tight budget? GoodAccess or Cloudflare's free tier.
  </p>
  <p>
    Big enterprise? You probably already know it's Cisco.
  </p>
  <p>
    Try the free trials. Actually test them. Your security shouldn't be a guess.
  </p>

  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/nordlayer"
      text="Start NordLayer Free Trial"
      variant="primary"
      program="nordlayer"
  />

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
